\caption(Fitted Cox survival curves for both groups at the same score.)
\alttext(Two descending step curves for groups 0 and 1. The estimate for group 1 is above group 0 at each event time.)

> Read the estimates

At score 2, group 1 has a lower fitted hazard than group 0. The table gives
model-based standard errors from the observed information. This small
cohort illustrates the calculations. Assess the proportional hazards
assumption and uncertainty before interpreting estimates from a real study.

The fitted baseline is the cumulative hazard at the training covariate
means. For another profile the model scales that hazard by its relative
hazard, then takes the exponential of the negative cumulative hazard to
obtain survival. Curves step at observed event times, and the last step
is carried forward beyond the last event.

- [Cox (1972), Regression Models and Life-Tables|https://doi.org/10.1111/j.2517-6161.1972.tb00899.x]
- [Efron (1977), The Efficiency of Cox's Likelihood Function for Censored Data|https://doi.org/10.1080/01621459.1977.10480613]
